{%- comment -%}
  Shared markup is prepended to every view before rendering, so this is where
  derived values, the vehicle diagram and reusable partials live.
{%- endcomment -%}

<style>
  /* Charge-limit tick on the progress track. The framework has no marker
     primitive, so this is the only custom rule the plugin adds. */
  .track { position: relative; }
  .charge-limit {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 3px;
    /* currentColor so the tick inverts with the framework in dark mode */
    background: currentColor;
  }

  /* Caliper under the track spanning what the last charge session added.
     Lives outside .track because that clips its overflow. */
  .charge-session {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    height: 6px;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    border-left: 2px solid currentColor;
    border-right: 2px solid currentColor;
    border-bottom: 2px solid currentColor;
  }
</style>

{%- comment -%} ---------- three states, not two ----------

  Every field arrives in one of three conditions, and the whole correctness of
  this screen rests on keeping them apart:

    true / a number   the sensor reported, and this is what it said
    false             the sensor reported, and the answer is no
    nil               the sensor did not report

  A view must never render nil as either of the other two. Rendering it as
  `false` invents an alarm — four flat tyres on a vehicle that simply has not
  checked in. Rendering it as `true`, or as `0`, is far worse: it produces a
  confident dashboard describing a vehicle nobody is talking to, and there is
  nothing on screen to say so. A dead integration used to render here as 0%
  charge, a "Low charge" badge and six green ticks.

  So nil propagates. Numbers become an em dash, checks become "?", and the
  status line says it does not know. Liquid's `default` filter is no help —
  it treats `false` as blank, so `false | default: true` returns true — and
  `nil` compares false against everything, so every test below is explicit.

{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign UNKNOWN = "&mdash;" -%}
{%- assign nothing = nil -%}

{%- comment -%} Only these two are safe to default: a body shape is cosmetic,
  and an absent charge snapshot is genuinely "no session on record" rather than
  a failed sensor. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- if body_style == nil %}{% assign body_style = "truck" %}{% endif -%}
{%- if last_charge_start_soc == nil %}{% assign last_charge_start_soc = 0 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if last_charge_end_soc == nil %}{% assign last_charge_end_soc = 0 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if moved_since_charge == nil %}{% assign moved_since_charge = true %}{% endif -%}
{%- if link_age_min == nil %}{% assign link_age_min = 0 %}{% endif -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- numeric display ---------- {%- endcomment -%}

{%- if soc == nil -%}
  {%- assign soc_known = false -%}
  {%- assign soc_display = UNKNOWN -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign soc_known = true -%}
  {%- assign soc_display = soc -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- if range == nil -%}
  {%- assign range_display = UNKNOWN -%}
  {%- assign range_unit = nothing -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign range_display = range -%}
  {%- assign range_unit = distance_unit -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- if cabin_temp == nil -%}
  {%- assign cabin_display = UNKNOWN -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign cabin_display = cabin_temp | append: "&deg;" | append: temp_unit -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- if odometer == nil -%}
  {%- assign odometer_display = UNKNOWN -%}
  {%- assign odometer_unit = nothing -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign odometer_display = odometer | number_with_delimiter -%}
  {%- assign odometer_unit = distance_unit -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- closures ----------

  Liquid has no array append — TRMNL's `push` filter silently returns an empty
  array — so lists are built as delimited strings and split at the end. Labels
  never contain a comma.

  Each label reads as a whole phrase, because they get joined into one sentence
  and "Front left" on its own does not say what is open.

  Note every test is `== true`. A nil closure is not open, but it is not closed
  either, and it must not be counted as one.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign d = "" -%}
{%- if door_fl == true %}{% assign d = d | append: "Front left door," %}{% endif -%}
{%- if door_fr == true %}{% assign d = d | append: "Front right door," %}{% endif -%}
{%- if door_rl == true %}{% assign d = d | append: "Rear left door," %}{% endif -%}
{%- if door_rr == true %}{% assign d = d | append: "Rear right door," %}{% endif -%}
{%- assign doors_open = d | split: "," -%}

{%- assign w = "" -%}
{%- if win_fl == true %}{% assign w = w | append: "Front left window," %}{% endif -%}
{%- if win_fr == true %}{% assign w = w | append: "Front right window," %}{% endif -%}
{%- if win_rl == true %}{% assign w = w | append: "Rear left window," %}{% endif -%}
{%- if win_rr == true %}{% assign w = w | append: "Rear right window," %}{% endif -%}
{%- assign windows_open = w | split: "," -%}

{%- assign s = "" -%}
{%- if frunk == true %}{% assign s = s | append: "Frunk," %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tonneau == true %}{% assign s = s | append: "Tonneau," %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tailgate == true %}{% assign s = s | append: "Tailgate," %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tunnel_l == true %}{% assign s = s | append: "Left gear tunnel," %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tunnel_r == true %}{% assign s = s | append: "Right gear tunnel," %}{% endif -%}
{%- assign storage_open = s | split: "," -%}

{%- assign open = doors_open | concat: windows_open | concat: storage_open -%}
{%- assign open_count = open | size -%}

{%- comment -%} How many closures never reported. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign closures_unknown = 0 -%}
{%- if door_fl == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if door_fr == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if door_rl == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if door_rr == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if win_fl == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if win_fr == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if win_rl == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if win_rr == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if frunk == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tonneau == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tailgate == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tunnel_l == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tunnel_r == nil %}{% assign closures_unknown = closures_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Something known-open outranks the gaps: we can say "the tailgate is up" even
  if three other sensors are quiet. But "all closed" may only be claimed when
  every closure actually said so.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if open_count > 0 -%}
  {%- assign closures_ok = false -%}
  {%- assign closure_summary = open | join: " &middot; " -%}
{%- elsif closures_unknown == 0 -%}
  {%- assign closures_ok = true -%}
  {%- assign closure_summary = "All closed" -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign closures_ok = nothing -%}
  {%- assign closure_summary = "Closures unknown" -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- health ---------- {%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Tyres collapse the same way: one flagged tyre is a fault whatever the others
  did, but "all fine" needs all four to have reported.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign tires_unknown = 0 -%}
{%- if tire_fl == nil %}{% assign tires_unknown = tires_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tire_fr == nil %}{% assign tires_unknown = tires_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tire_rl == nil %}{% assign tires_unknown = tires_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tire_rr == nil %}{% assign tires_unknown = tires_unknown | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}

{%- if tire_fl == false or tire_fr == false or tire_rl == false or tire_rr == false -%}
  {%- assign tires_ok = false -%}
{%- elsif tires_unknown == 0 -%}
  {%- assign tires_ok = true -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign tires_ok = nothing -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Two separate tallies, because they mean opposite things. `issue_count` is what
  the driver should act on. `unknown_count` is what the screen cannot vouch for.
  Collapsing the second into either the first or into silence is precisely the
  bug this plugin exists not to have.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign issue_count = 0 -%}
{%- if locked == false %}{% assign issue_count = issue_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if closures_ok == false %}{% assign issue_count = issue_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tires_ok == false %}{% assign issue_count = issue_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if battery_12v_ok == false %}{% assign issue_count = issue_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if fluids_ok == false %}{% assign issue_count = issue_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if software_ok == false %}{% assign issue_count = issue_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}

{%- assign unknown_count = 0 -%}
{%- if locked == nil %}{% assign unknown_count = unknown_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if closures_ok == nil %}{% assign unknown_count = unknown_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if tires_ok == nil %}{% assign unknown_count = unknown_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if battery_12v_ok == nil %}{% assign unknown_count = unknown_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if fluids_ok == nil %}{% assign unknown_count = unknown_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}
{%- if software_ok == nil %}{% assign unknown_count = unknown_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  A real fault outranks a gap, but it must not hide one: the small views have
  room for a single line, and "1 issue" alone would quietly imply the other five
  checks came back clean. The trailing "4?" is what stops that.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if issue_count > 0 -%}
  {%- assign health_state = "bad" -%}
  {%- assign health_summary = "issue" | pluralize: issue_count -%}
  {%- if unknown_count > 0 -%}
    {%- assign health_summary = health_summary | append: " &middot; " | append: unknown_count | append: "?" -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- elsif unknown_count > 0 -%}
  {%- assign health_state = "unknown" -%}
  {%- assign health_summary = unknown_count | append: " unknown" -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign health_state = "good" -%}
  {%- assign health_summary = "All good" -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- freshness ----------

  Two different questions, and conflating them is how a dead feed looks alive.

  `updated_at` is how old the readings are — the newest change among the values
  on screen. It is deliberately not the render time, because a timestamp that
  moves on every push makes every payload unique, defeats TRMNL's deduplication
  and forces a pointless e-ink redraw. It can be legitimately old: a vehicle
  asleep in a garage really has not changed.

  `link_age_min` is how long since anything reported at all, measured in Home
  Assistant and bucketed so a healthy link is always 0. That is the one that
  means "broken", and it is why a dead feed can announce itself: the bucket
  steps up, the payload changes, and TRMNL re-renders a screen that says so.

  The age below is computed at render time, which matters because TRMNL
  re-renders on its own refresh interval. If Home Assistant itself dies nothing
  pushes at all — but the clock keeps moving, the age keeps growing, and the
  screen degrades on its own rather than holding a confident stale image.

{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign now_ts = 'now' | date: '%s' | plus: 0 -%}
{%- assign updated_ts = 0 -%}
{%- if updated_at and updated_at != "" -%}
  {%- assign updated_ts = updated_at | date: '%s' | plus: 0 -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- if updated_ts > 0 -%}
  {%- assign updated_known = true -%}
  {%- assign data_age_min = now_ts | minus: updated_ts | divided_by: 60 -%}
  {%- if data_age_min < 0 %}{% assign data_age_min = 0 %}{% endif -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign updated_known = false -%}
  {%- assign data_age_min = 0 -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Stale when the feed says it stopped reporting, or when nothing has moved for
  a day, or when there is no timestamp to judge by at all.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign stale_min = 0 -%}
{%- assign stale = false -%}
{%- if link_age_min >= 30 -%}
  {%- assign stale = true -%}
  {%- assign stale_min = link_age_min -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if updated_known == false -%}
  {%- assign stale = true -%}
{%- elsif data_age_min >= 1440 -%}
  {%- assign stale = true -%}
  {%- if data_age_min > stale_min %}{% assign stale_min = data_age_min %}{% endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- if stale_min >= 1440 -%}
  {%- assign stale_age = stale_min | divided_by: 1440 | append: "d" -%}
{%- elsif stale_min >= 60 -%}
  {%- assign stale_age = stale_min | divided_by: 60 | append: "h" -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign stale_age = stale_min | append: "m" -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- if updated_known == false -%}
  {%- assign stale_label = "No data" -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign stale_label = "No update " | append: stale_age -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The timestamp always carries a date, and that is not belt-and-braces — it is
  the only thing standing between a dead feed and a convincing wall display.

  TRMNL deduplicates at the data layer: an unchanged payload is dropped *before*
  the render step, so the template is never re-evaluated and the clock above
  never advances on its own. If Home Assistant stops pushing entirely, the last
  image simply stays on the wall exactly as it was rendered. Whatever that image
  said is what someone will read a week later.

  So a bare "Updated 4:25 PM" is a trap: rendered while perfectly fresh, frozen
  by the outage, and indistinguishable from live at a glance the following
  afternoon. "Updated Thu Jul 30, 4:25 PM" survives being frozen, because it was
  never relative in the first place.

  The relative badge below still has a job — it appears whenever a render *does*
  happen, which is exactly when Home Assistant is alive and reporting that the
  integration behind it is not.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if updated_known == false -%}
  {%- assign updated_fmt = "" -%}
  {%- assign updated_short = "" -%}
  {%- assign updated_label = "No data" -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign updated_fmt = updated_at | date: "%a %b %-d, %-I:%M %p" -%}
  {%- assign updated_short = updated_at | date: "%b %-d, %-I:%M %p" -%}
  {%- assign updated_label = "Updated " | append: updated_fmt -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- status line ----------

  What the vehicle is doing, and where. Most specific state wins. `location` is a
  zone name from Home Assistant, blank when the vehicle is somewhere unnamed.

  "Parked" is a claim, not a default: it may only be said once the signals that
  would contradict it have actually reported. When they have not, the screen
  says so instead of picking the reassuring answer.

{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if location and location != "" -%}
  {%- assign at_place = " at " | append: location -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign at_place = "" -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The signature of the whole vehicle-state feed being down: nothing on the
  charge or security side reported at all.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if soc == nil and locked == nil and charging == nil and plugged_in == nil -%}
  {%- assign blackout = true -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign blackout = false -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Service mode goes first, ahead of the no-data branch, because it is a fact the
  vehicle reported and not an absence of one. A vehicle can sit at a service
  centre for a week: the state is correct, the readings are simply old, and the
  screen must say both. Staleness is carried by its own badge and by the
  timestamp, so "In service" is never blurred into "we lost contact" — telling a
  known state apart from an unknown one is the same discipline as telling an
  unknown one apart from zero.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if service_mode == true -%}
  {%- assign status_line = "In service" -%}
  {%- assign mode = "In service" -%}
{%- elsif blackout -%}
  {%- assign status_line = "No vehicle data" -%}
  {%- assign mode = "No data" -%}
{%- elsif driving == true -%}
  {%- assign status_line = "Driving" -%}
  {%- assign mode = "Driving" -%}
{%- elsif charging == true -%}
  {%- assign status_line = "Charging" | append: at_place -%}
  {%- assign mode = "Charging" -%}
{%- elsif plugged_in == true -%}
  {%- assign status_line = "Plugged in" | append: at_place -%}
  {%- assign mode = "Plugged in" -%}
{%- elsif driving == nil or charging == nil or plugged_in == nil -%}
  {%- assign status_line = "Status unknown" -%}
  {%- assign mode = "Unknown" -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign status_line = "Parked" | append: at_place -%}
  {%- assign mode = "Parked" -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- charge ----------

  A low-charge warning needs a real number behind it. It used to fire on a
  missing sensor, because absent coerced to 0 and 0 is less than 15 — the badge
  that shouted loudest was the one with no data at all.

{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if soc_known and soc <= 15 and charging != true -%}
  {%- assign low_charge = true -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign low_charge = false -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Charge rate is only worth the pixels while current is flowing — and it is the
  one figure that goes dark on its own, because it is the only thing here still
  sourced from Rivian's retired live-session query. Nil drops the segment; it
  never renders as 0 kW.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if charging == true and charge_power != nil and charge_power > 0 -%}
  {%- assign charge_power_known = true -%}
  {%- assign mode_detail = mode | append: " &middot; " | append: charge_power | append: " kW" -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign charge_power_known = false -%}
  {%- assign mode_detail = mode -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- last charge session ----------

  Plenty of people charge on a timer or a cheap-rate window, so once a session
  ends the useful question is "how much did it actually put in?". Shown only
  while the answer still describes the charge in the battery: not charging now,
  and not driven since. One drive and it is history, so it disappears.

  `last_charge_kwh_est` marks a figure derived from the SOC delta and the pack
  capacity rather than measured at the charger — the measured value needs the
  retired live-session query. Estimates are shown with a "~" so nobody reads
  one as metered.

{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign last_charge_delta = last_charge_end_soc | minus: last_charge_start_soc -%}
{%- if charging != true and moved_since_charge == false and last_charge_delta > 0 -%}
  {%- assign show_last_charge = true -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign show_last_charge = false -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- assign last_charge_fmt = last_charge_at | date: "%a %-I%p" -%}
{%- if last_charge_kwh == nil -%}
  {%- assign last_charge_kwh_label = "" -%}
{%- elsif last_charge_kwh_est == true -%}
  {%- assign last_charge_kwh_label = last_charge_kwh | prepend: "~" | append: " kWh" -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign last_charge_kwh_label = last_charge_kwh | append: " kWh" -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} Charge ETA as "1h 35m" / "45m", or nothing at all if unknown. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- if charge_eta_min == nil -%}
  {%- assign eta_known = false -%}
  {%- assign eta = UNKNOWN -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign eta_known = true -%}
  {%- assign eta_h = charge_eta_min | divided_by: 60 -%}
  {%- assign eta_m = charge_eta_min | modulo: 60 -%}
  {%- if eta_h > 0 -%}
    {%- assign eta = eta_h | append: "h " | append: eta_m | append: "m" -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- assign eta = eta_m | append: "m" -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The charge line under the bar. Assembled here so all four views agree, and so
  each segment can drop out independently as its source goes dark.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture charge_line -%}
  {%- if charging == true -%}
    {%- if charge_power_known %}{{ charge_power }} kW{% endif -%}
    {%- if charge_power_known and eta_known %} &middot; {% endif -%}
    {%- if eta_known %}{{ eta }} to limit{% endif -%}
    {%- unless charge_power_known or eta_known %}Charging{% endunless -%}
  {%- elsif show_last_charge -%}
    Last charge +{{ last_charge_delta }}%
    {%- if last_charge_kwh_label != "" %} &middot; {{ last_charge_kwh_label }}{% endif -%}
    {%- if last_charge_at and last_charge_at != "" %} &middot; {{ last_charge_fmt }}{% endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  `vehicle_label` is the plugin's custom field, so a value set in TRMNL wins over
  the name Home Assistant sends. Liquid's `default` treats "" as blank, so an
  empty field falls through. A name is not telemetry — falling back here invents
  nothing.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign display_name = vehicle_label | default: vehicle | default: model | default: "Rivian" -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- vehicle diagram ----------

  Top-down schematic, nose up, captured once so every view can drop it in with
  {{ vehicle_svg }}. Open closures are drawn the way they actually open rather
  than just shaded: doors swing out on their hinge, the frunk lifts past the
  nose, the tailgate drops past the tail, gear tunnels swing out the side. A
  window that is down leaves a solid gap in the door, and the tonneau's slats
  disappear when it is open. A flagged tire fills its wheel.

  The viewBox is wider and taller than the body so swung-open panels have room
  to extend outside the silhouette without clipping.

  A solid outline is itself a claim — it says "everything you see is shut". When
  any closure or tyre never reported, the whole silhouette switches to a dashed
  stroke, so the drawing stops asserting a state it cannot know. Every panel
  test is `== true` for the same reason: nil must not draw as closed.

{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if closures_unknown == 0 and tires_unknown == 0 -%}
  {%- assign diagram_dash = "" -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign diagram_dash = "5 4" -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- capture vehicle_svg -%}
<svg viewBox="0 0 180 340" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
     stroke-dasharray="{{ diagram_dash }}"
     style="width:100%;height:100%;display:block">
  <!-- wheels; a flagged tire fills -->
  <rect x="29" y="124" width="9" height="36" rx="4" fill="{% if tire_fl == false %}currentColor{% else %}none{% endif %}"/>
  <rect x="142" y="124" width="9" height="36" rx="4" fill="{% if tire_fr == false %}currentColor{% else %}none{% endif %}"/>
  <rect x="29" y="234" width="9" height="36" rx="4" fill="{% if tire_rl == false %}currentColor{% else %}none{% endif %}"/>
  <rect x="142" y="234" width="9" height="36" rx="4" fill="{% if tire_rr == false %}currentColor{% else %}none{% endif %}"/>

  <!-- body: 104 x 288 is the R1T's real footprint ratio -->
  <rect x="38" y="26" width="104" height="288" rx="16" stroke-width="2.5"/>

  <!-- light bar across the nose -->
  <path d="M56 35 H124" stroke-width="3"/>

  <!-- frunk; lifts past the nose when open -->
  <rect x="50" y="44" width="80" height="44" rx="6"/>
  {% if frunk == true %}<rect x="52" y="4" width="76" height="22" rx="4" fill="currentColor"/>{% endif %}

  <!-- glasshouse; kept to two shapes so it stays readable at ~150px wide -->
  <path d="M52 94 H128 L120 112 H60 Z" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <rect x="62" y="114" width="56" height="78" rx="4" stroke-width="1.5"/>

  <!-- doors; the leaf swings out on its hinge when open. Left doors rotate
       clockwise and right doors anti-clockwise to swing away from the body. -->
  <rect x="39" y="116" width="21" height="37" rx="3"/>
  <rect x="39" y="153" width="21" height="37" rx="3"/>
  <rect x="120" y="116" width="21" height="37" rx="3"/>
  <rect x="120" y="153" width="21" height="37" rx="3"/>
  {% if door_fl == true %}<rect x="33" y="116" width="5" height="37" rx="2" fill="currentColor" transform="rotate(40 38 116)"/>{% endif %}
  {% if door_rl == true %}<rect x="33" y="153" width="5" height="37" rx="2" fill="currentColor" transform="rotate(40 38 153)"/>{% endif %}
  {% if door_fr == true %}<rect x="142" y="116" width="5" height="37" rx="2" fill="currentColor" transform="rotate(-40 142 116)"/>{% endif %}
  {% if door_rr == true %}<rect x="142" y="153" width="5" height="37" rx="2" fill="currentColor" transform="rotate(-40 142 153)"/>{% endif %}

  <!-- windows; a lowered one leaves a gap in the door -->
  <rect x="52" y="121" width="6" height="27" rx="2" stroke-width="1.5" fill="{% if win_fl == true %}currentColor{% else %}none{% endif %}"/>
  <rect x="52" y="158" width="6" height="27" rx="2" stroke-width="1.5" fill="{% if win_rl == true %}currentColor{% else %}none{% endif %}"/>
  <rect x="122" y="121" width="6" height="27" rx="2" stroke-width="1.5" fill="{% if win_fr == true %}currentColor{% else %}none{% endif %}"/>
  <rect x="122" y="158" width="6" height="27" rx="2" stroke-width="1.5" fill="{% if win_rr == true %}currentColor{% else %}none{% endif %}"/>

  {% if body_style == "truck" %}
    <!-- gear tunnel; swings out the side -->
    <rect x="39" y="210" width="21" height="18" rx="3"/>
    <rect x="120" y="210" width="21" height="18" rx="3"/>
    {% if tunnel_l == true %}<rect x="19" y="211" width="19" height="16" rx="3" fill="currentColor"/>{% endif %}
    {% if tunnel_r == true %}<rect x="142" y="211" width="19" height="16" rx="3" fill="currentColor"/>{% endif %}

    <!-- bed; the tonneau's slats vanish when it is open -->
    <rect x="44" y="232" width="92" height="64" rx="4"/>
    {% unless tonneau == true %}
      <path d="M48 248 H132 M48 264 H132 M48 280 H132" stroke-width="1.5"/>
    {% endunless %}
  {% else %}
    <rect x="44" y="210" width="92" height="86" rx="4"/>
  {% endif %}

  <!-- tailgate; drops past the tail when open -->
  <rect x="44" y="298" width="92" height="12" rx="3"/>
  {% if tailgate == true %}<rect x="46" y="314" width="88" height="22" rx="4" fill="currentColor"/>{% endif %}
</svg>
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Front-view mark for the title bar: the vehicle's face, with the light bar that
  reads even at 28px. Not the Rivian trademark — set the plugin's Logo URL field
  to use an official asset instead.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture vehicle_mark -%}
<svg class="image" viewBox="0 0 36 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
     style="height:100%;width:auto;display:block">
  <rect x="1.5" y="2.5" width="33" height="19" rx="6"/>
  <rect x="5" y="7" width="4.5" height="10" rx="2.25" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"/>
  <rect x="26.5" y="7" width="4.5" height="10" rx="2.25" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"/>
  <path d="M11.5 12 H24.5"/>
</svg>
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- partials ---------- {%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  A labelled figure. `unit` renders smaller and tight against the value, and is
  omitted when the value is unknown — "— mi" reads like a measurement.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% template stat %}
<div class="flex flex--col flex--left gap--none">
  <span class="label label--small">{{ label }}</span>
  <div class="flex flex--row flex--bottom gap--[3px]">
    <span class="value value--small value--tnums">{{ value }}</span>
    {% if unit %}<span class="label label--small">{{ unit }}</span>{% endif %}
  </div>
</div>
{% endtemplate %}

{%- comment -%}
  A health check, in three states. Passing checks stay quiet; failing ones invert
  so the eye lands on them first; and a check whose sensor never reported shows
  an outlined "?" — visibly not a tick, visibly not an alarm.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% template check %}
{% if ok == nil %}
  <span class="label label--small label--outline">?&nbsp;{{ label }}</span>
{% elsif ok %}
  <span class="label label--small">&#10003;&nbsp;{{ label }}</span>
{% else %}
  <span class="label label--small label--filled">&#10007;&nbsp;{{ label }}</span>
{% endif %}
{% endtemplate %}
